    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72777.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "be talking about how to increase wages in this country. How to make sure that work pays more. But the conclusion of all these hearings has been this is a good program. It's an important program. Now, the Republicans have a political agenda with regard to SNAP. They don't like it. They've demonized the program. They claim that people on the program are lazy or somehow undeserving. And they have talked about block ranting the program. They have talked about cutting the program even more. I mean, they've succeeded in cutting it in the last Farm Bill and prior to that as well. They have talked about putting more hurdles in place, more work requirements for people, more hoops for people to jump through. And so, you know, their approach to this is, you know, is kind of from a point of hostility. And I'm not saying the program is perfect, but I'm saying it's a damn good program, and it serves an important purpose. I mean, food ought to be right in this country for everybody. We need to, we can't live without it. And I've seen too many people in my congressional district and around the country who are hungry. I have had families come into my office who don't know where the next meal is going to come from. I have been at soup kitchens, I have been at homeless shelters, I have been at food banks, I have talked to veterans who are hungry, I have talked to, I've seen children in classrooms who come to school on a Monday, haven't had anything to eat over the weekend. And they kind of stare off into a day because they can't focus. And that food for that child, everybody's important as a textbook and they've been out of that child to learn. You know, I think we ought to be talking about how do we strengthen the program, and we also ought to have a full discussion on how do you end hunger in America. We have close to 47 million people who are hungry in this country. That is unacceptable. That is shameful. We ought to be ashamed of that fact. And yet we don't talk about that. So SNAP is one of the tools to deal with hunger, but we need a more holistic discussion. And I continue to believe that, you know, I wish this president would have done it. I hope the next president will do a White House conference on food, nutrition and hunger and actually bring everybody together including farmers, including teachers, doctors, nutritionists, I mean, heads of food banks, anybody who's interested, bring them together and let us come up with a holistic plan that will lend hunger in this country once and for all.",
