  {
    "episode_id": 72777,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72777.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "Well, first of all, when people talk about blockwriting the program, that's basically code for cutting people off the program, giving stake the ability to spend their monies on whatever they want to. And I think when it comes to making sure people have food, we have to be certain that the monies we're sending actually go for that purpose. So I think that's a mistake. I think we ought to be talking as well about how do we increase the benefit to a point where people can afford to put food on the table for the entire month. We also should be talking about nutrition and security as well. It's not enough just to give people junk. We want to make sure people are getting food that's actually good for them. Food is medicine. I've talked to nutritionists who lament over the amount of young people. I've talked about young teenagers who have or battling with obesity and diabetes and high blood pressure, all of it related to what they eat. If we could do something about that, you not only give that young child a better future, you also save the system a whole boatload of money and avoidable health care costs. Hunger costs us a lot of money. And one of the things we're not very good about in the Agriculture Committee, or even in Congress in general, is kind of looking at things holistically. We can't raise one more penny for this program. But if you look at the issue more holistically, you'd say, well, if we raised this a little bit here, we could save a whole bunch over here. And we could save a whole bunch over here. We need to look at this from a bigger lens. uh... you know so i don't have all the answers but i do know that that in various parts of the country in addition to snap they have been programs that have been successful it that have been forward-thinking that have helped try to deal with the issue of hunger and food insecurity in a way that is kind of more comprehensive that's why i want this white house carter bring the best practices together under one roof and let's see whether we can replicate them. Let's see whether we can actually do something that once and for all and hunger in this country.",
