    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72777.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 11,
    "turn_text": "Well, on one hand, I mean, I understand the impulse. You know, we want to make sure that people have access to healthier and one nutritious foods. on the other hand the reality is is you know a lot of the stores we're talking about you know are these kind of mom and pop stores or convenience stores or have you that one don't have the space or don't have the capacity for storage and that you know couldn't can't comply with some of these new regulations that u s d a is putting out and for a lot of people especially a lot of poor people those the only places within get food sometimes people don't have a car if you live in a rural area you don't have a car you you know you're you're you're limited, or if you live in a rural area in the closest major supermarket, which 30 miles away, the cost of gas to and from is significant, but you tend to rely on that smaller store. Yes, we want to make sure that healthier and more nutritious foods are accessible to more people, and I'm all for that. But I think the way the rule has been drafted, my fear, is that it would mean that a lot of people would lose access to food period. and i think that would be a mistake so that's why i mean i think in this one area both democrats and republicans actually kind of we're seeing the same tune to say the u.s.d.a. you know you better go back to the drawing board.",
