    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72777.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 16,
    "turn_text": "I think it would be a stupid idea to separate ag from nutrition in a farm bill unless you don't want a farm bill. I don't know how you get a farm bill if you take the nutrition component out of the bill. And I hear some of my Republican colleagues who suggest that saying, well, more money should be going to farmers. If programs like SNAP don't benefit farmers, people can only buy food with SNAP. Who grows food? Farmers grow food. So these nutrition programs, they help individuals who are struggling, but they're also purchasing foods that are grown here in the United States of America. You know, I'm always in awe that so many of the food banks, especially here in Massachusetts, make a special effort to buy local foods to support our local farmers. You know, they want to support a strong agriculture here in Massachusetts. And they also appreciate the fact that so many of our farmers are unbelievably generous, you know, with some of their excess food and with gleaning efforts, and",
