    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72777.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 18,
    "turn_text": "and on and on. I mean, farmers have been in the forefront of this battle to end hunger forever and thank god they are because hunger would be much worse in this country if it weren't for the generosity of our farmers but you try to convince somebody who lives in a city that they should be supporting subsidies for this or that or that crop of this crop that's a tough sell but the support of bill and those and they're willing to be reasonable on these other aspects of the Farm Bill. If they can actually show their constituents and they can prove to themselves that there's a benefit for the people they represent. And by the way, I would also say that people who live in rural areas should not want them to touch either because the nutrition parts of the Farm Bill are even more important for rural areas. We percentage-wise, more people rely on SNAP in our rural areas than in our urban areas. So when people talk about SNAP, they always say, oh, this is a city issue. No, it's a city issue, and it is a big issue in rural areas in this country. And the challenges of hunger in rural areas are even more difficult to try to overcome. I think farm bills are important. And I think if you separate them, you're not going to get a farm bill.",
