  {
    "episode_id": 72777,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72777.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "Well, my answer to that would be I didn't like the legislation to begin with. I thought that The cleanest thing for us to do would simply just require mandatory GMO labeling. I mean, I can buy a candy bar in London, and it tells me that it contains GMOs, but I can buy the same candy bar in Boston, and it says nothing. You know, I think if people, you know, want to know what's in their food, they ought to know what's in their food. And I'm not anti-GMO, I eat GMOs. My kids eat GMOs. But I don't see what the big fuss is over mandatory labeling. quite frankly, and I've had this discussion with industry that I think that their insistence to fight this labeling has made people suspicious. Like, what are you hiding? If there's nothing to hide, then why? What's the big deal with labeling? My worry about what was done in this compromise legislation is that two of the options don't work. One is you could call it into an 800 number. Let's be honest, if you go shopping, I mean, you could just walk around with your cell phone, and dial 1-800 for every product you buy to find out what's in it, or the other is this kind of this barcode, which you could put your smartphone up to, and it could tell you what's in the package. When I'm in Washington, I shop at the Harris Theater, not too far from the capital. And the one thing that I can tell you about the Harris Theater is that when I walk in, my cell phone has a work. I can't even call my wife to find out whether I'm reading the grocery list correctly. There's no service. So, I mean, if the goal is to make it confusing or to even give people the ability not to know what's in their food, then that legislation, as it's written, actually provides a pathway for that. Now, it depends on how the Department of Agriculture issues the regulations, and so some of us are going to work with them to try to make sure that whatever it is, it's as transparent as possible, and it's easy to understand. And it's user-friendly. And we should just do it. And look, I, you know, all this resistance, I would just tell you, you know, over a period of time will be overcome because people have got to demand to know it's in their foods. And so, you know, if there's some pickups along the way, I think in the long term, there will be mandatory GMO labeling. But I just wish we could just get to it sooner and get this behind us. And make all this less confusing both for the people who produce the food and for the people who buy the food",
