  {
    "episode_id": 72777,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72777.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "Well, thank you for having me and again I'm just going to go back to what we talked about at the beginning and that's this issue of hunger in the importance of American agriculture in combating it. I mean, not only in terms of the food that we produce, we did a hearing in the Agriculture Committee about food waste and about a lot of the food that's grown on farms that is discarded for various reasons, but it's perfectly good to eat. We need to put together an infrastructure to help our small and medium-sized farms of particular be able to recapture that food and get it to people who need it, whether it's in food banks or homeless shelters or in our schools or senior centers uh... you know nobody in this country uh... should ever go hungry and uh... and unfortunately uh... the reality in this country is that tens of millions of our fellow citizens are hungry and uh... and i also believe that you know uh... american farmers in this country can do more to you know protect our national security around the world uh... then although weapons and bombs were selling to every country that will pay for them.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
